---
/**
 * /programmes/enterprise/ — the page for a sponsor or an L&D lead who is
 * thinking about a group rather than about themselves.
 *
 * THE ROADMAP ROW THIS PAGE IS WRITTEN AGAINST
 *
 *   "Sponsor/L&D exploring a group. Needs domain relevance and practical
 *    scope; explain readiness, group learning and coordination. Evidence:
 *    approved scope and industry-default facts. Next step: enterprise enquiry."
 *
 * The reader here is not the reader of the cohort page. They are not asking
 * whether they personally would enjoy it; they are asking whether it is
 * defensible to put eight people's time and a budget line behind it, and
 * whether it will survive contact with their industry. So the page leads with
 * scope and coordination rather than with the subject, which they have usually
 * already accepted by the time they arrive.
 *
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * AN ENTERPRISE ENQUIRY IS NOT AN APPLICATION, AND THIS PAGE MUST NOT BLUR IT
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * The brief is explicit: "An enterprise enquiry is never counted as an
 * application", and "participant nominations stored separately — an
 * organisation order is not eight individual applications". That distinction is
 * a data-model rule, but it starts here, in the copy: a page that invites a
 * sponsor to "apply for your team" produces exactly the collapse the pipeline
 * is built to prevent. The action on this page is a conversation, never an
 * application.
 *
 * WHAT MUST NOT BE ADDED
 *
 *   * Hours. "30 live hours" belongs to the OPEN COHORT. The delivered copy
 *     says only that an enterprise programme "normally brings 8–10
 *     participants together with a schedule agreed for the group". Anything
 *     more precise is invented.
 *   * Fees, packages, tiers, day rates or a delivery guarantee. None are
 *     approved; a pricing table here would be the first place a prospect
 *     quotes back at us.
 *   * Client names, logos, case studies or headcounts trained. There are none.
 *   * A response-time promise — the operating guide forbids it in public copy.
 *   * A second enterprise form. The enquiry route already exists on the cohort
 *     page at /#team, wired to the pipeline with its own route, its own record
 *     and its own idempotency key. A duplicate here would be a second form
 *     definition to keep in step, which is how the two drift.
 */
import PracticeLayout from '../../layouts/PracticeLayout.astro';
import { SITE_ORIGIN } from '../../data/facts';
import { EXPLORES } from '../../data/cohort-copy';

const title = 'Enterprise learning for a team — The Living Craft';
const description =
  'A group programme in agentic system design for one organisation: normally 8–10 participants, on a schedule agreed for the group, beginning with your learning objective and industry context.';

/**
 * The only structured data on this page, and it is here because the crumb
 * trail above the heading is visibly rendered. The roadmap allows breadcrumbs
 * "only when the visible content supports it"; a BreadcrumbList describing a
 * trail nobody can see is markup written for a crawler alone.
 */
const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': `${SITE_ORIGIN}/programmes/enterprise/#breadcrumbs`,
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Programmes', item: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/programmes/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Enterprise learning' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<PracticeLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="/programmes/enterprise/"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/programmes/">Programmes</a>
      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Enterprise learning</span>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Enterprise learning</p>
      <h1>A programme for <em>one team</em></h1>
      <p class="credential">
        An enterprise programme normally brings <strong>8–10 participants</strong>
        together with a schedule agreed for the group. It begins with your learning
        objective, the experience of the people taking part and your industry context.
        It is a separate purchase and a separate scoping conversation from the open
        cohort.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#team">Start the team conversation&nbsp;<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="mailto:apply@thelivingcraft.ai">apply@thelivingcraft.ai</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">
        An enquiry is a conversation, not an application and not an order.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── who and when ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">When a group is the right shape</p>
      <h2>When the decisions are <em>shared</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose stack">
      <p>
        A group programme earns its cost when several people will be arguing about the
        same design and need the same vocabulary to do it. That is usually a team
        putting an agent behind something that acts — a system that moves money, changes
        a record or contacts a customer — where the awkward questions are going to be
        settled in a review meeting rather than by one engineer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One person, not a team?</strong> The
        <a class="textlink" href="/">open cohort</a> is the better answer,
        and an employer can fund that place.
        <strong>A live decision rather than capability?</strong> That is
        <a class="textlink" href="/advisory/">advisory</a> — reviewing an architecture
        you already have, not building capability across a group.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── the work ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">What a group works on</p>
      <h2>The same five areas, <em>argued out loud</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose intro">
      <p>
        Take the house example. A returns assistant reads a policy and recommends a
        refund; give it a tool that can issue the refund and the system can move money.
        In a group, the interesting part is that people disagree about what evidence
        should permit the action — and that disagreement is the material. Everyone
        leaves able to say why the boundary is where it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        The example is illustrative. No customer incident is claimed, and nothing in it
        requires your organisation to disclose anything.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="tlist">
      {
        EXPLORES.map((item, i) => (
          <li>
            <span class="n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  {/* ── domain relevance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Domain relevance</p>
      <h2>Industry context, <em>without your internals</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose stack">
      <p>
        <strong>Industry-specific practice is the default.</strong> The work is set in
        the kind of system your team actually builds — the constraints, the review
        culture and the failure modes of your sector — without anyone handing over
        confidential architecture to make that possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Company-specific work needs a separate conversation</strong> about scope
        and permission. It is not refused; it is simply not something to arrange by
        email in an enquiry thread, because it changes what material is shared, who may
        see it and what is agreed in writing first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please do not attach confidential company material to an enquiry. Your learning
        objective, the experience of the participants and your industry are enough to
        have a useful first conversation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── readiness and coordination ───────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Readiness and coordination</p>
      <h2>What a first conversation <em>needs from you</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cards2">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Bring three things</h3>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Learning objective</span><span class="dv">What the group should be able to do afterwards</span></div>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Participant experience</span><span class="dv">Where the group sits on system design today</span></div>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Industry context</span><span class="dv">The sector and the constraints it works under</span></div>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Approximate group size</span><span class="dv">Normally 8–10 participants</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>How coordination works</h3>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Schedule</span><span class="dv">Agreed for the group, not fixed in advance</span></div>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Nominations</span><span class="dv">Handled as one organisation’s group, not as individual applications</span></div>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Attendance and access</span><span class="dv">Confirmed before the group starts</span></div>
        <div class="detail-row"><span class="dk">Certificate</span><span class="dv">None</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="pending">
      <h3>Facts this page is waiting on</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Live hours, delivery format and how many sessions an enterprise group runs.
          <span class="who">Sunil to approve the scope before publication</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Fees, payment schedule, cancellation and rescheduling terms.
          <span class="who">Sunil with finance; nothing is published until agreed</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Contracting entity and the terms a purchase order would sit under.
          <span class="who">The owner — the same facts <a href="/terms">terms</a> is waiting on</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </section>

  {/* ── next step ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next step</p>
      <h2>Start the <em>scoping conversation</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose stack">
      <p>
        The enterprise enquiry form asks for your organisation, your role, what the team
        needs to learn, an approximate group size and your industry. It is recorded as
        an enquiry from an organisation — never as a set of individual applications —
        and the reply picks up from what you wrote rather than asking for it again.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also write directly to
        <a class="textlink" href="mailto:apply@thelivingcraft.ai">apply@thelivingcraft.ai</a>.
        No response-time commitment is published, because none has been agreed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#team">Start the team conversation&nbsp;<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/programmes/">Compare all three routes</a>
    </div>
    <p class="revised">
      Written <time datetime="2026-09-11">11 September 2026</time>. Scope and terms
      pending Sunil’s factual approval.
    </p>
  </section>
</PracticeLayout>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .panel h3 {
    font-size: var(--size-5);
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
    color: var(--text-loud);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .panel .detail-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }
  .panel .dv {
    max-width: 30ch;
  }

  .pending {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  /* The pending block's own links: it sits on the uncertain ground, so an
     inherited ink underline would disappear into it. */
  .pending a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .revised {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    color: var(--text-quiet);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .panel .detail-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--space-1);
    }
    .panel .dv {
      text-align: left;
      max-width: none;
    }
  }
</style>
